#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "6c8b136c-7944-599f-9399-19641212fcbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9537" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7e61f72ed7488fd490f42ff519316ed2e87d3c997d64e2cc6b7af36f951dbb5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                               0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/06/063836a4780ecb4be6e8c6149a422e3c8e9f2770304e659a8116691b91e3d7f1.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/704915d2852e1654aa31d17bd2dfb07a3022fe3d8b597f37bf98bd5af2de1d1b.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/id #uuid "16605c7c-c238-51ef-84e6-d5b5d58e1539" :question/math-academy-id "e-9539" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))=-y\\,i+x\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "1e15ae93-0a63-506a-a0ae-b15e035f0494" :question/math-academy-id "e-9543" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =( e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ that is \n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} =  e^y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b17ed1b6-595c-59c7-8401-b1ee92464b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177802} {:answer/id #uuid "c14cdb8f-7db5-51d4-a45b-623698993c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177803} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e696e7-68c8-5d46-acac-bf9e9f41c601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177804} {:answer/id #uuid "fce36292-ea6a-5a99-bd42-52e2820d9c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177805} {:answer/id #uuid "12489ce9-a97c-5c72-9081-4e50f57d87b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177806}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8776e1-fe8b-540d-a3d2-bcaf7a9551dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177801}] :question/id #uuid "1d320327-1d15-55df-b5dd-771fb0fd1831" :question/math-academy-id "q-125394" :question/problem "![](images/27/270e72ebedac3741e1b7086021b16dc0975ae9852d402673c8044a67f8f774fb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F$, shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∮_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0$\n2. $∮_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0$\n3. $∮_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d38863e-8c06-597a-afb7-14092ea52376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257025} {:answer/id #uuid "302f71d0-ddcf-5469-b62a-8627f2f507ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257207} {:answer/id #uuid "49295a90-64c7-5cf1-b3f6-9c51f6fc366a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257421} {:answer/id #uuid "567c01fa-62a0-5797-ace6-12ba30bdbc9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257526} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff9a7ed-4b4d-561a-afaa-30bbfcb2f18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "302f71d0-ddcf-5469-b62a-8627f2f507ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257207} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4623d71-f288-52db-9325-b4d7a1224d2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2353653c-eb1d-5d31-b3c0-eed1015199de" :question/math-academy-id "q-125664" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}+3x)\\,i+(2xy+3)\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II and III are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
